---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { loadGuide } from '../lib/content';

const guide = loadGuide();
const t = guide.translation;
const counts = { ai: 0, edited: 0, reviewed: 0, none: 0 };
for (const c of guide.chapters) {
  for (const k of c.concepts) {
    if (k.status === 'reviewed') counts.reviewed++;
    else if (k.status === 'edited') counts.edited++;
    else if (k.status === 'ai-draft') counts.ai++;
    else counts.none++;
  }
}
---

<Base title="About this guide" current="about">
  <div class="page">
    <header class="page-head">
      <p class="eyebrow"><span>About</span></p>
      <h1 class="chapter-title">How this guide was made</h1>
    </header>
    <div class="prose about">
      <h2>Sources</h2>
      <p>
        The guide follows one series of recorded classes: <strong>Rabbi Markel’s advanced series</strong> on <em>Shaar HaYichud</em>. Each
        recording was transcribed by machine, and each line of the Hebrew text was matched by machine to the moment in the class where it is
        read. That matching is what the ▶ buttons use. Where it is uncertain, the button is marked <span class="approx">approx.</span>
      </p>
      <p>
        The explanation of each passage was then drafted by AI from the part of the class that follows the reading of that passage: his
        explanation, his analogies, and the questions he answered. It is meant to say only what he said. Logistics, side conversations and
        unclear stretches were left out; unclear stretches are listed in a “For the reviewer” box on screen.
      </p>
      <p>
        The opening of chapter 1 (§1–20) has no recording in this series, so those paragraphs appear with text and translation only.
      </p>

      <h2>What is and is not reviewed</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Nothing in the explanations has been reviewed by Rabbi Markel yet.</strong> Of {guide.chapters.reduce((n, c) => n + c.concepts.length, 0)}
        sections, {counts.ai} are AI drafts, {counts.edited} edited, {counts.reviewed} reviewed{counts.none > 0 ? `, and ${counts.none} have no explanation` : ''}.
        The Hebrew text and the English translation are reproduced as published. Topic lists for the classes and the source transcripts are
        machine-generated and unedited.
      </p>

      <h2>Attribution and licences</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>English translation:</strong> {t ? `“${t.versionTitle}”, licence ${t.license}` : 'Rabbi Amiram Markel'}, from
          {' '}{t ? <a href={t.source} rel="noopener">{t.source.replace(/^https?:\/\//, '')}</a> : 'neirot.com'}, via
          <a href="https://www.sefaria.org/The_Gate_of_Unity" rel="noopener">Sefaria</a>. Footnotes are the translator’s.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Hebrew text:</strong> from the Shaar HaYichud class archive (shaar-hayichud-sections{guide.sectionsCommit ? `, commit ${guide.sectionsCommit.slice(0, 7)}` : ''}). Its licence is not yet confirmed.
        </li>
        <li><strong>Audio:</strong> Rabbi Markel’s classes, streamed from media.shaarhayichudresources.com.</li>
        <li><strong>Transcripts and topic lists:</strong> machine-generated (Sofer.ai), shown unedited.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Printing</h2>
      <p>
        Every chapter prints as a booklet: running heads, page numbers and the draft notice in the footer (in Chrome and Edge; other browsers
        print without the running heads). Printing follows the view you have chosen. In <em>Explanation only</em> the prose is set in two
        columns. The whole booklet is on <a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}booklet/`}>one page</a> for printing in one go.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
